# ---- GOLDEN FIXTURE (bridge tile) ----
# resetMode=loadvm -> restore the INTERNAL 'golden' snapshot: X + ContrAlto 2
# at the Alto Executive on the Non-Programmer's Disk, untouched, exactly as a
# cold boot leaves it.
#
# NOT inside Bravo, and that was a real decision. Bravo is the headline — the
# first WYSIWYG word processor — and it takes about half a minute to load, so
# baking the golden inside it would put every visitor straight into it with no
# wait. plus4 shipped exactly that and 10ae428 reversed it: a visitor who
# arrives mid-application has no idea what it is, how they got there or how to
# leave. The Executive is the machine's honest empty state AND its launcher, so
# the choice of application lives in the exhibit UI instead — the SPA's alto
# keyboard profile carries ?, BRAVO, DRAW and LAUREL, one Executive command
# each. docs/guests/alto.md records the argument in full.
SH_RESET_MODE=loadvm
SH_GOLDEN_SNAPSHOT=golden
SH_FIXTURE_DESC=Xerox Alto II XM (1973) at the Alto Executive, the machine's own command prompt, exactly as a cold boot leaves it and with nothing typed into it: the two banner lines "-- XEROX Alto Executive/11 ----------- Date and Time Unknown - 1609 Pages --" and "--- OS Version 20/16 --- Alto 0#42# --- NoName --- Non-Prog.BFS ------------" across the top, then "// eventBooted" and a waiting ">" prompt. Faintly blue-white text on black, in the Alto's own proportional serif face, on a PORTRAIT 606x808 page display — the only exhibit in the collection whose screen is taller than it is wide. ContrAlto 2 renders that into a 608-wide bitmap and the kiosk X root is 608x808 to match exactly, so there is no letterbox and no surround anywhere in the capture. The disk is the Non-Programmer's Disk, so BRAVO, DRAW and LAUREL are all one Executive command away and the SPA's alto on-screen keyboard sends each from a labelled button. Three-button mouse (RED/YELLOW/BLUE = host left/middle/right), absolute; keyboard paced at two Alto fields.

# ---- INPUT ----
# ABSOLUTE POINTER, WITH NO CALIBRATION, AND THAT IS UNUSUAL FOR AN EMULATOR
# BRIDGE. ContrAlto's UI calls MouseMoveAbsolute() — it warps the Alto's cursor
# to the host pointer rather than feeding it deltas — so a stock usb-tablet
# lands where the visitor points. Measured on the clone, uncalibrated:
# (300,400) -> (302,402), (100,700) -> (101,700), (600,800) -> (602,802).
#
# THREE buttons, and all three do different things. The Alto's RED/YELLOW/BLUE
# are host left/middle/right, and Bravo is where that is visible: RED selects a
# character, YELLOW the whole word, BLUE extends the selection to the pointer.
# A click needs real dwell (~400 ms) like the other Xerox tiles.
#
# ---- KEY PACING ----
# ContrAlto samples its keyboard once per Alto field, so the playbook §5.1 rule
# applies and the numbers were measured rather than copied. A 20-character line,
# explicit press/release pairs: 16/16 ms landed 15 of 20; 33/33, 66/66 and
# 120/120 all landed 20 of 20. One field is 33 ms, so this is two fields.
# (Note for whoever tunes the neighbours: the 400 ms hold that Darkstar and
# Dwarf need is NOT a fleet-wide constant — this emulator does not want it.)
SH_KEY_MIN_HOLD_MS=66
SH_KEY_MIN_GAP_MS=66

# ---- IDLE PAUSE ----
# The Alto never idles: its microcode display task repaints 30 fields a second
# whatever is on screen, so ContrAlto costs ~170-190 % of a core and ~180 MB RSS
# whether or not anybody is looking. The daemon's default 60-second freeze is
# therefore left ON, explicitly, so nobody copies amiga's SH_IDLE_PAUSE_SECS=0
# here. Pause is not loadvm: guest RAM and device state are untouched and the
# next visitor thaws at the same prompt, sub-second.
SH_IDLE_PAUSE_SECS=60
